* The soles of this armor were originally meant to be gold, as it can be seen in [[Alex Ross]]' covers to {{cl|Iron Man Vol 5 3}} and #6. They were colored inconsistently in the interior artwork, and Ross started coloring them red with the cover of {{cl|Iron Man Vol 5 11}}. In comics such as {{cl|Captain America/Iron Man Vol 1 1}}, the soles were even recolored from gold to red between the preview pages and the final version, as it can be seen [https://imgur.com/a/Qj6qfZ5 here]. In {{cl|Invincible Iron Man Vol 5}}, the soles were consistently colored gold.
* Despite the suit's trademark see-through eyeslits, it has been drawn with the conventional glowing eyes in several appearances outside Iron Man's main title, and notably in series such as {{cl|Avengers Vol 8}} or {{cl|A.X.E.: Judgment Day Vol 1}}. {{cl|Iron Man Vol 5 22}} has showcased that the armor can make its eyes glow, but appears to be reserved to instances when the armor is in some kind of scanning mode.
| Trivia                  = * This suit is the second vintage style designed by [[Alex Ross]], the previous being the [[Iron Man Armor Model 16]].
* This armor was originally designed and pitched by Ross in 2018 as a reimagining of the [[Iron Man Armor Model 4|Classic Armor]] with its aesthetics revamped to modern standards. At the time, Marvel already had [[Anthony Stark (Earth-616)|Iron Man]]'s path planned out, but they let Ross use the redesign for a [[:File:Invincible Iron Man Vol 4 600 Ross Virgin Variant.jpg|variant cover]] for {{cl|Invincible Iron Man Vol 4 600}}.{{cite|ref=true|url=https://www.facebook.com/alexrossart/videos/892380747957278|title=Iron Man by Alex Ross|work=Facebook|first=Alex|last=Ross|date=August 20, 2020|accessdate=September 19, 2020}} This new armor eventually debuted in the pages of the comics in {{cl|Iron Man Vol 5 1}}, released two years after its inception and its first appearance in the variant cover.
* The armor's practical segmentation is inspired by the aesthetic of the [[Iron Man Armor (Earth-199999)|Iron Man Armor]] from the [[Earth-199999|Marvel Cinematic Universe]].<ref>Ross, A. and Kidd, C., 2018. ''Marvelocity: The Marvel Comics Art of Alex Ross''. New York: Knopf.</ref>
* One of the key distinctions of this armor's design is that it's meant to be drawn with an extremely reflective chrome finish.{{cite|ref=true|url=https://www.facebook.com/alexrossart/posts/new-iron-man-designs/2660944830790569/|title=New- Iron Man designs|work=Facebook|first=Alex|last=Ross|date=June 12, 2020|accessdate=September 19, 2020}}
* {{cl|Iron Man Vol 5}} writer [[Christopher Cantwell]] has stated that his one piece of input in terms of the armor was being able to see Tony's eyes within the helmet. It was extremely important to him to drop the glow in Iron Man's eyeslits in order to humanize the character.{{cite|ref=true|url=https://www.syfy.com/syfywire/christopher-cantwell-goes-back-to-basics-in-marvels-iron-man-1|title=Co-creator of Halt and Catch Fire on how his TV work helped him reboot Marvel Comics' Iron Man|work=Syfy Wire|first=Jeff|last=Spry|date=August 3, 2020|accessdate=September 19, 2020}} This distinctive feature was already present in Alex Ross' concept art for this armor.{{cite|ref=true|url=https://www.facebook.com/alexrossart/posts/new-iron-man-designs/2660944830790569/|title=New- Iron Man designs|work=Facebook|first=Alex|last=Ross|date=June 12, 2020|accessdate=September 19, 2020}}
* In addition to the visibility of the eyes, it was important for Cantwell for the armor to feel like pieces that Stark puts on, which is reflected with the armor's first suit-up sequence in {{cl|Iron Man Vol 5 1}}, in which it can be seen the way the sections of the armor connect.{{cite|ref=true|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FQfVtuT_UIo|title=Iron Man Spotlight with Christopher Cantwell|work=YouTube|last=Geek History Lesson|date=January 31, 2021|accessdate=April 10, 2021}} 
